window.LEARNING_MAP = {"stages":[{"id":"s1","title":"One request, one database","summary":"The physics every later decision rests on: what a network call and a disk read cost, how an index and a transaction work, what a clean API returns, and how you see a service is healthy. Everything later assumes these."},{"id":"s2","title":"Correctness under concurrency and retries","summary":"How to keep data right when two writers race and when a client retries: isolation levels, idempotency, compatible contracts, and who is allowed to do what. These come before resilience patterns because retries are only safe once operations are idempotent."},{"id":"s3","title":"Resilience, performance and operations","summary":"Keeping one service fast and up under real load and partial failure: timeouts, retries, breakers, caching, capacity limits, and the observability and release practice that catch problems early. It builds on the correctness of stage 2."},{"id":"s4","title":"Distributed data","summary":"What changes when data lives on more than one machine: the CAP and latency trade-offs, consistency models, replication, consensus and sharding. You need single-node transactions and caching first to see what is lost and why."},{"id":"s5","title":"Distributed services and change","summary":"Coordinating work across services without distributed transactions: events, the outbox, sagas and CQRS, plus the gateway and migration patterns that let a system change while it runs. It combines idempotency, transactions and replication from earlier stages."},{"id":"s6","title":"Security and data platform architecture","summary":"System-wide architecture decisions: threat models, service identity and zero trust, and how an analytics platform is built from columnar files, table formats, ingestion and reconciliation. Both lean on the transaction, CDC and identity ideas already covered."},{"id":"s7","title":"Capstone designs","summary":"Three end-to-end designs (payments, order fulfilment, a lakehouse) that combine the earlier stages under real constraints. Work them as interview rehearsals: state the requirements, pick the trade-offs, and name what would change your answer."}],"paths":[{"id":"engineer","title":"Senior backend engineer","summary":"Implementation depth: query and transaction correctness, idempotent APIs, resilience and performance mechanics, distributed data internals, event-driven integration, and the payment and order capstones. Data platforms and security architecture are covered only where an engineer builds them.","steps":["01-distributed-systems.html#c1","06-performance-scale.html#c1","03-apis-contracts.html#c1","03-apis-contracts.html#c5","02-data-storage.html#c3","02-data-storage.html#c1","02-data-storage.html#c2","06-performance-scale.html#c3","03-apis-contracts.html#c4","06-performance-scale.html#c5","06-performance-scale.html#c6","05-reliability-observability.html#c3","05-reliability-observability.html#c2","05-reliability-observability.html#c1","07-security.html#c1","02-data-storage.html#c4","02-data-storage.html#c5","01-distributed-systems.html#c8","04-microservices-patterns.html#p5","03-apis-contracts.html#c9","03-apis-contracts.html#c3","03-apis-contracts.html#c2","03-apis-contracts.html#c7","03-apis-contracts.html#c8","07-security.html#c3","07-security.html#c4","07-security.html#c6","04-microservices-patterns.html#p3","04-microservices-patterns.html#p2","04-microservices-patterns.html#p7","03-apis-contracts.html#c6","05-reliability-observability.html#c7","05-reliability-observability.html#c6","03-apis-contracts.html#c10","06-performance-scale.html#c4","06-performance-scale.html#c8","06-performance-scale.html#c7","06-performance-scale.html#c2","06-performance-scale.html#c10","05-reliability-observability.html#c4","05-reliability-observability.html#c5","05-reliability-observability.html#c8","05-reliability-observability.html#c10","01-distributed-systems.html#c2","01-distributed-systems.html#c3","01-distributed-systems.html#c4","01-distributed-systems.html#c7","02-data-storage.html#c6","01-distributed-systems.html#c5","01-distributed-systems.html#c10","01-distributed-systems.html#c6","02-data-storage.html#c7","06-performance-scale.html#c9","04-microservices-patterns.html#p8","04-microservices-patterns.html#p6","02-data-storage.html#c10","02-data-storage.html#c8","01-distributed-systems.html#c9","04-microservices-patterns.html#p10","04-microservices-patterns.html#p1","04-microservices-patterns.html#p9","04-microservices-patterns.html#p4","04-microservices-patterns.html#p11","05-reliability-observability.html#c9","07-security.html#c5","07-security.html#c7","07-security.html#c8","07-security.html#c9","08-data-platforms.html#c1","08-data-platforms.html#c2","08-data-platforms.html#c11","payment-system.html#c1","payment-system.html#c2","payment-system.html#c3","payment-system.html#c4","payment-system.html#c5","payment-system.html#c8","order-fulfilment.html#c1","order-fulfilment.html#c2","order-fulfilment.html#c4","order-fulfilment.html#c5","order-fulfilment.html#c8"]},{"id":"architect","title":"Solutions architect","summary":"Decision depth: the trade-offs behind datastores, consistency and service boundaries, security architecture from threat model to zero trust, the full data platform, and all three capstones. Low-level tuning mechanics are left to the engineer path.","steps":["01-distributed-systems.html#c1","06-performance-scale.html#c1","03-apis-contracts.html#c1","03-apis-contracts.html#c5","02-data-storage.html#c3","02-data-storage.html#c1","06-performance-scale.html#c5","05-reliability-observability.html#c3","05-reliability-observability.html#c2","05-reliability-observability.html#c1","07-security.html#c1","02-data-storage.html#c4","02-data-storage.html#c5","01-distributed-systems.html#c8","04-microservices-patterns.html#p5","03-apis-contracts.html#c9","03-apis-contracts.html#c3","03-apis-contracts.html#c2","03-apis-contracts.html#c7","03-apis-contracts.html#c8","07-security.html#c3","07-security.html#c4","07-security.html#c6","04-microservices-patterns.html#p3","04-microservices-patterns.html#p2","04-microservices-patterns.html#p7","03-apis-contracts.html#c6","05-reliability-observability.html#c7","05-reliability-observability.html#c6","03-apis-contracts.html#c10","06-performance-scale.html#c4","06-performance-scale.html#c8","05-reliability-observability.html#c4","05-reliability-observability.html#c8","05-reliability-observability.html#c10","01-distributed-systems.html#c2","01-distributed-systems.html#c3","01-distributed-systems.html#c4","01-distributed-systems.html#c7","02-data-storage.html#c6","01-distributed-systems.html#c10","01-distributed-systems.html#c6","02-data-storage.html#c7","06-performance-scale.html#c9","04-microservices-patterns.html#p8","02-data-storage.html#c9","04-microservices-patterns.html#p6","02-data-storage.html#c10","02-data-storage.html#c8","01-distributed-systems.html#c9","04-microservices-patterns.html#p10","04-microservices-patterns.html#p1","04-microservices-patterns.html#p9","04-microservices-patterns.html#p4","04-microservices-patterns.html#p11","04-microservices-patterns.html#p12","05-reliability-observability.html#c9","07-security.html#c2","07-security.html#c5","07-security.html#c7","07-security.html#c8","07-security.html#c9","07-security.html#c10","08-data-platforms.html#c1","08-data-platforms.html#c2","08-data-platforms.html#c3","08-data-platforms.html#c4","08-data-platforms.html#c5","08-data-platforms.html#c6","08-data-platforms.html#c7","08-data-platforms.html#c8","08-data-platforms.html#c9","08-data-platforms.html#c10","08-data-platforms.html#c11","08-data-platforms.html#c12","08-data-platforms.html#c13","payment-system.html#c1","payment-system.html#c2","payment-system.html#c3","payment-system.html#c4","payment-system.html#c5","payment-system.html#c6","payment-system.html#c7","payment-system.html#c8","order-fulfilment.html#c1","order-fulfilment.html#c2","order-fulfilment.html#c3","order-fulfilment.html#c4","order-fulfilment.html#c5","order-fulfilment.html#c6","order-fulfilment.html#c7","order-fulfilment.html#c8","lakehouse-platform.html#c1","lakehouse-platform.html#c5","lakehouse-platform.html#c2","lakehouse-platform.html#c3","lakehouse-platform.html#c4","lakehouse-platform.html#c6","lakehouse-platform.html#c7","lakehouse-platform.html#c8"]}],"concepts":[{"id":"01-distributed-systems.html#c1","page":"01-distributed-systems.html","pageTitle":"01 · Distributed systems","title":"The Eight Fallacies of Distributed Computing","difficulty":2,"connects":["04-microservices-patterns.html#p2","04-microservices-patterns.html#p3","01-distributed-systems.html#c8"],"references":[],"stage":"s1","level":"foundation","summary":"Eight false assumptions about networks, such as zero latency and a reliable network, that cause most distributed-system bugs.","why":"Timeouts, retries, breakers and idempotency all exist because of these fallacies. An engineer who forgets them ships calls with no timeout; an architect who forgets them draws diagrams that fail on the first partition.","prereqs":[],"practice":"Write a small client and server, then use a proxy such as Toxiproxy (or a sleep and random drop in the server) to add latency and dropped responses. List which fallacy each failure shows and add a timeout that fixes the hang."},{"id":"01-distributed-systems.html#c2","page":"01-distributed-systems.html","pageTitle":"01 · Distributed systems","title":"CAP Theorem","difficulty":2,"connects":["01-distributed-systems.html#c3","01-distributed-systems.html#c7","01-distributed-systems.html#c6"],"references":[],"stage":"s4","level":"foundation","summary":"During a network partition a replicated system must choose between consistency (refuse) and availability (answer, possibly stale).","why":"Every replicated datastore makes this choice, and it decides what users see during a failure. Misreading it as \"pick two always\" leads to wrong datastore choices.","prereqs":["01-distributed-systems.html#c1"],"practice":"Run a three-node etcd cluster and a three-node Cassandra or Redis cluster in Docker. Partition one node with network rules and record which one refuses writes and which one returns stale reads."},{"id":"01-distributed-systems.html#c3","page":"01-distributed-systems.html","pageTitle":"01 · Distributed systems","title":"PACELC Theorem","difficulty":3,"connects":["01-distributed-systems.html#c2","01-distributed-systems.html#c4","01-distributed-systems.html#c7"],"references":[],"stage":"s4","level":"core","summary":"An extension of CAP: when there is no partition, a replicated system still trades latency against consistency.","why":"Partitions are rare but the latency cost of consistency is paid on every request. This is the trade-off behind most datastore and replica decisions.","prereqs":["01-distributed-systems.html#c2","06-performance-scale.html#c1"],"practice":"Using Cassandra (or ScyllaDB) with three nodes and added network delay, measure read and write latency at consistency ONE, QUORUM and ALL. Classify two databases you know as PA/EL or PC/EC with a reason."},{"id":"01-distributed-systems.html#c4","page":"01-distributed-systems.html","pageTitle":"01 · Distributed systems","title":"Consistency Models","difficulty":3,"connects":["01-distributed-systems.html#c2","01-distributed-systems.html#c3","02-data-storage.html#c5"],"references":[],"stage":"s4","level":"core","summary":"The ladder of guarantees from linearizable through sequential, causal and read-your-writes to eventual consistency.","why":"Choosing the weakest model that still protects your invariants saves latency and cost. Choosing too weak a model causes \"I just saved it and it vanished\" bugs.","prereqs":["01-distributed-systems.html#c2"],"practice":"Set up Postgres with an async read replica and add replication delay. Show a read-your-writes violation from the replica, then fix it by routing a user's reads to the primary for a short window after they write."},{"id":"01-distributed-systems.html#c5","page":"01-distributed-systems.html","pageTitle":"01 · Distributed systems","title":"Logical & Vector Clocks","difficulty":4,"connects":["01-distributed-systems.html#c4","04-microservices-patterns.html#p9"],"references":[],"stage":"s4","level":"advanced","summary":"Lamport timestamps give a consistent order of events; vector clocks also detect which events were concurrent.","why":"Wall clocks across machines cannot order events reliably, which breaks last-write-wins and conflict detection. This is the machinery behind Dynamo-style stores and CRDTs.","prereqs":["01-distributed-systems.html#c4"],"practice":"Implement vector clocks for three simulated nodes exchanging messages. Generate concurrent updates to one key, detect them as concurrent, and resolve them with a merge function."},{"id":"01-distributed-systems.html#c6","page":"01-distributed-systems.html","pageTitle":"01 · Distributed systems","title":"Consensus & Raft","difficulty":4,"connects":["01-distributed-systems.html#c2","01-distributed-systems.html#c7","04-microservices-patterns.html#p8"],"references":[],"stage":"s4","level":"advanced","summary":"How a cluster agrees on one ordered log while a majority is alive, as in Raft: leader election, log replication and safety.","why":"Locks, configuration, leader election and strongly consistent databases all rest on consensus. Knowing its majority and latency costs prevents misuse.","prereqs":["01-distributed-systems.html#c2","01-distributed-systems.html#c10","02-data-storage.html#c6"],"practice":"Run a three-node etcd cluster. Kill the leader and time the new election, then kill a second node and show writes stop without a majority. Use etcd to hold a lease-based lock for two competing workers."},{"id":"01-distributed-systems.html#c7","page":"01-distributed-systems.html","pageTitle":"01 · Distributed systems","title":"Quorums · R + W > N","difficulty":3,"connects":["01-distributed-systems.html#c2","01-distributed-systems.html#c3","01-distributed-systems.html#c6"],"references":[],"stage":"s4","level":"core","summary":"With N replicas, writing to W and reading from R where R + W > N guarantees every read overlaps the latest write.","why":"Quorums are the tunable knob behind leaderless stores. Wrong values give either stale reads or writes that block on one slow node.","prereqs":["01-distributed-systems.html#c2","01-distributed-systems.html#c4"],"practice":"Write a small simulation of N=5 replicas with random failures and delays. For several (R, W) pairs, measure stale-read rate and availability, and explain the result with R + W > N."},{"id":"01-distributed-systems.html#c8","page":"01-distributed-systems.html","pageTitle":"01 · Distributed systems","title":"Idempotency · the practical answer","difficulty":2,"connects":["04-microservices-patterns.html#p5","01-distributed-systems.html#c9","04-microservices-patterns.html#p10"],"references":[],"stage":"s2","level":"foundation","summary":"Making an operation safe to repeat: retries produce the same effect as one execution, through natural idempotency or a stored key and result.","why":"Retries are unavoidable over a network, so without idempotency every timeout risks a duplicate effect. It turns partial failures from correctness bugs into latency.","prereqs":["01-distributed-systems.html#c1"],"practice":"Write a \"charge\" endpoint, call it, and drop the response with a proxy so the client retries. Show the double charge, then make the endpoint idempotent with a client-chosen key and show the retry returns the original result."},{"id":"01-distributed-systems.html#c9","page":"01-distributed-systems.html","pageTitle":"01 · Distributed systems","title":"Exactly-Once is a lie","difficulty":4,"connects":["01-distributed-systems.html#c8","04-microservices-patterns.html#p10","04-microservices-patterns.html#p6"],"references":[],"stage":"s5","level":"advanced","summary":"Exactly-once delivery over a lossy network is impossible; exactly-once effects come from at-least-once delivery plus idempotent processing.","why":"Vendors and designs that promise exactly-once hide the duplicate case. Knowing the real guarantee prevents double-charging and double-counting.","prereqs":["01-distributed-systems.html#c8","04-microservices-patterns.html#p6"],"practice":"Build a consumer that adds payment amounts to a balance. Redeliver messages by crashing before the offset commit and show double counting, then fix it by storing processed message ids in the same transaction as the balance update."},{"id":"01-distributed-systems.html#c10","page":"01-distributed-systems.html","pageTitle":"01 · Distributed systems","title":"Failure Detectors & Heartbeats","difficulty":3,"connects":["01-distributed-systems.html#c6","04-microservices-patterns.html#p2","04-microservices-patterns.html#p8"],"references":[],"stage":"s4","level":"core","summary":"Deciding whether a node is dead from heartbeats and timeouts, ideally with an adaptive suspicion level such as phi accrual.","why":"Every timeout is a guess: too short gives false failovers during GC pauses, too long leaves calls hanging. Failover, discovery and leader election all depend on it.","prereqs":["01-distributed-systems.html#c1"],"practice":"Write a heartbeat sender and a monitor. Add random pauses and jitter, compare a fixed-timeout detector with a phi accrual detector, and count false alarms and detection time for each."},{"id":"02-data-storage.html#c1","page":"02-data-storage.html","pageTitle":"02 · Data & storage","title":"Indexes · B-tree vs LSM","difficulty":3,"connects":["02-data-storage.html#c2","02-data-storage.html#c5"],"references":[],"stage":"s1","level":"foundation","summary":"How B-tree indexes (fast reads, in-place updates) and LSM trees (fast sequential writes, compaction) store and find data.","why":"Index choice drives read and write cost, and it explains why some stores suit write-heavy workloads. Engineers need it to fix slow queries; architects need it to choose a datastore.","prereqs":[],"practice":"Load 5 million rows into Postgres, query by a non-indexed column, add a B-tree index and compare timings and table size. Then insert 1 million rows into SQLite and into RocksDB (or LevelDB) and compare write throughput."},{"id":"02-data-storage.html#c2","page":"02-data-storage.html","pageTitle":"02 · Data & storage","title":"Query Plans · the EXPLAIN reflex","difficulty":2,"connects":["02-data-storage.html#c1"],"references":[],"stage":"s1","level":"foundation","summary":"Reading EXPLAIN ANALYZE output to see how the database actually ran a query: scans, joins, row estimates and time per step.","why":"Most \"the database is slow\" problems are bad plans, not bad hardware. Guessing leads to wrong indexes and wasted scaling spend.","prereqs":["02-data-storage.html#c1"],"practice":"Write three slow queries on a seeded Postgres table (missing index, function on an indexed column, stale statistics). Read each EXPLAIN ANALYZE plan, fix the cause, and record before and after timings."},{"id":"02-data-storage.html#c3","page":"02-data-storage.html","pageTitle":"02 · Data & storage","title":"ACID & the I in it","difficulty":2,"connects":["02-data-storage.html#c4","02-data-storage.html#c5","01-distributed-systems.html#c4"],"references":[],"stage":"s1","level":"foundation","summary":"The four transaction guarantees: atomic all-or-nothing, consistent invariants, isolated from other transactions, durable after commit.","why":"Every money or inventory flow depends on these guarantees, and isolation is the one most often misunderstood. Knowing what ACID does and does not give you decides when you need extra locking or a different design.","prereqs":[],"practice":"In Postgres, transfer money between two accounts inside a transaction and kill the session mid-way. Confirm nothing was half-applied. Then add a CHECK (balance >= 0) constraint and show it rejects an overdraft."},{"id":"02-data-storage.html#c4","page":"02-data-storage.html","pageTitle":"02 · Data & storage","title":"Isolation Levels & Anomalies","difficulty":4,"connects":["02-data-storage.html#c5","02-data-storage.html#c3","01-distributed-systems.html#c4"],"references":[],"stage":"s2","level":"core","summary":"The SQL isolation levels and the anomalies each allows: dirty reads, non-repeatable reads, phantoms, lost updates and write skew.","why":"Default isolation is weaker than most engineers assume, so concurrency bugs pass tests and fail under load. Money, inventory and booking flows depend on choosing the right level or lock.","prereqs":["02-data-storage.html#c3"],"practice":"Run two writers against one Postgres row at READ COMMITTED and reproduce a lost update. Fix it with SELECT ... FOR UPDATE, then with an atomic UPDATE, then reproduce write skew (two on-call doctors) and fix it with SERIALIZABLE."},{"id":"02-data-storage.html#c5","page":"02-data-storage.html","pageTitle":"02 · Data & storage","title":"MVCC · readers don't block writers","difficulty":4,"connects":["02-data-storage.html#c4","01-distributed-systems.html#c5"],"references":[],"stage":"s2","level":"advanced","summary":"Multi-version concurrency control: the database keeps row versions so readers see a snapshot and do not block writers.","why":"MVCC explains snapshot behaviour, table bloat and why long transactions hurt vacuum. It also underlies table formats and time travel in data platforms.","prereqs":["02-data-storage.html#c4"],"practice":"In Postgres, open a long REPEATABLE READ transaction, update rows from another session, and inspect xmin/xmax and pg_stat_user_tables dead tuples. Show vacuum cannot reclaim them until the long transaction ends."},{"id":"02-data-storage.html#c6","page":"02-data-storage.html","pageTitle":"02 · Data & storage","title":"Replication topologies","difficulty":3,"connects":["01-distributed-systems.html#c7","01-distributed-systems.html#c2","02-data-storage.html#c7"],"references":[],"stage":"s4","level":"core","summary":"Leader-follower, multi-leader and leaderless replication, and what each costs in consistency, conflicts and failover.","why":"The topology decides read scaling, write latency across regions, and what is lost on failover. Multi-leader brings conflicts that need a resolution rule.","prereqs":["01-distributed-systems.html#c4","01-distributed-systems.html#c7"],"practice":"Run Postgres with one primary and one streaming replica. Measure replica lag under write load, then promote the replica after killing the primary and count any lost commits with async replication."},{"id":"02-data-storage.html#c7","page":"02-data-storage.html","pageTitle":"02 · Data & storage","title":"Sharding strategies","difficulty":4,"connects":["02-data-storage.html#c6","01-distributed-systems.html#c7","04-microservices-patterns.html#p4"],"references":[],"stage":"s4","level":"advanced","summary":"Splitting data across machines by range, hash or a lookup directory, and using consistent hashing to make resharding cheaper.","why":"The shard key is very hard to change later; a wrong one gives permanent hot shards or expensive cross-shard queries. Sharding is a last resort with large operational cost.","prereqs":["02-data-storage.html#c6","02-data-storage.html#c1"],"practice":"Write a small router that distributes 1 million keys over 4 nodes by modulo hash and by consistent hashing with virtual nodes. Add a fifth node and compare how many keys move."},{"id":"02-data-storage.html#c8","page":"02-data-storage.html","pageTitle":"02 · Data & storage","title":"Change Data Capture (CDC)","difficulty":3,"connects":["04-microservices-patterns.html#p10","04-microservices-patterns.html#p6","02-data-storage.html#c10"],"references":[],"stage":"s5","level":"core","summary":"Change Data Capture reads the database's own change log (WAL, binlog) and turns every committed change into an event.","why":"CDC feeds caches, search and analytics without dual writes or polling. It needs care with replication slots, schema changes and ordering.","prereqs":["02-data-storage.html#c10","02-data-storage.html#c6"],"practice":"Run Postgres, Kafka and Debezium with Docker Compose. Capture inserts, updates and deletes from one table, inspect the before and after images, and consume them to keep a Redis cache in sync."},{"id":"02-data-storage.html#c9","page":"02-data-storage.html","pageTitle":"02 · Data & storage","title":"Polyglot persistence · the right tool","difficulty":2,"connects":["02-data-storage.html#c8","02-data-storage.html#c10"],"references":[],"stage":"s4","level":"core","summary":"Using different datastores for different workloads, added only when a measured bottleneck needs a new tool.","why":"Each extra datastore adds operations work, sync problems and failure modes. Architects are judged on avoiding needless ones as much as on adding the right one.","prereqs":["02-data-storage.html#c1","01-distributed-systems.html#c3"],"practice":"Take a sample app with orders, product search and analytics. Write a one-page decision record: what Postgres alone can do (including full-text and JSONB), the measured trigger for adding search or an OLAP store, and the sync cost."},{"id":"02-data-storage.html#c10","page":"02-data-storage.html","pageTitle":"02 · Data & storage","title":"The dual-write problem","difficulty":3,"connects":["04-microservices-patterns.html#p10","02-data-storage.html#c8","01-distributed-systems.html#c9"],"references":[],"stage":"s5","level":"core","summary":"Writing to a database and publishing to a broker as two separate steps, so a crash between them leaves them inconsistent.","why":"This bug causes silent drift between systems that retries cannot fix. Recognising it in designs is a core senior skill.","prereqs":["02-data-storage.html#c3","04-microservices-patterns.html#p6"],"practice":"Write a service that inserts an order then publishes to a broker. Kill the process between the two calls with a debug flag and show the order exists with no event. Keep this for the outbox exercise."},{"id":"03-apis-contracts.html#c1","page":"03-apis-contracts.html","pageTitle":"03 · APIs & contracts","title":"REST vs gRPC vs GraphQL","difficulty":2,"connects":["03-apis-contracts.html#c2","03-apis-contracts.html#c3","04-microservices-patterns.html#p4"],"references":[],"stage":"s1","level":"foundation","summary":"The three common API styles: REST for broad reach, gRPC for fast typed internal calls, GraphQL for client-shaped queries.","why":"The API style fixes caching, tooling, browser support and coupling for years. Picking GraphQL or gRPC for the wrong audience creates cost that is hard to undo.","prereqs":[],"practice":"Expose one \"get order with its lines and customer\" operation three ways (REST, gRPC, GraphQL) in a small service. Compare payload size, number of round trips, and how you would cache each at a CDN."},{"id":"03-apis-contracts.html#c2","page":"03-apis-contracts.html","pageTitle":"03 · APIs & contracts","title":"API versioning","difficulty":3,"connects":["03-apis-contracts.html#c3","03-apis-contracts.html#c5"],"references":[],"stage":"s2","level":"core","summary":"Where an API carries its version (URL path or header) and the rule that a major version never breaks its clients.","why":"Once external clients exist, every breaking change costs their time and your trust. A clear versioning and sunset policy lets you evolve without emergency migrations.","prereqs":["03-apis-contracts.html#c3"],"practice":"Take a small REST API, introduce a breaking change to a response field, and ship it as /v2 while /v1 keeps working. Write a deprecation header and a one-page sunset timeline for v1."},{"id":"03-apis-contracts.html#c3","page":"03-apis-contracts.html","pageTitle":"03 · APIs & contracts","title":"Schema evolution","difficulty":3,"connects":["03-apis-contracts.html#c2","02-data-storage.html#c8"],"references":[],"stage":"s2","level":"core","summary":"Changing schemas without breaking readers or writers: backward and forward compatibility rules, as built into Protobuf and Avro.","why":"Producers and consumers deploy at different times, so a breaking field change takes down the side that has not upgraded. Contracts, events and data pipelines all depend on this discipline.","prereqs":["03-apis-contracts.html#c1"],"practice":"Define a Protobuf message, serialise data with v1, then add, rename and remove fields in v2. Decode across versions both ways and record which changes broke compatibility. Add a CI check with buf breaking."},{"id":"03-apis-contracts.html#c4","page":"03-apis-contracts.html","pageTitle":"03 · APIs & contracts","title":"Pagination · cursor vs offset","difficulty":2,"connects":["02-data-storage.html#c1","02-data-storage.html#c2"],"references":[],"stage":"s1","level":"foundation","summary":"Offset pagination skips rows by count; cursor pagination resumes after the last row seen using an indexed sort key.","why":"Offset pages get slower with depth and skip or repeat rows when data changes. Public APIs that start with offsets are hard to migrate later.","prereqs":["02-data-storage.html#c1"],"practice":"Seed 2 million rows and implement both offset and cursor pagination on (created_at, id). Time page 1 and page 5,000 for each, and insert rows during a scan to show offset duplicates."},{"id":"03-apis-contracts.html#c5","page":"03-apis-contracts.html","pageTitle":"03 · APIs & contracts","title":"Error model design","difficulty":2,"connects":["04-microservices-patterns.html#p2","03-apis-contracts.html#c6","05-reliability-observability.html#c4"],"references":[],"stage":"s1","level":"foundation","summary":"A consistent error format (status code, stable machine code, human detail, trace id), as in RFC 7807 Problem Details.","why":"Clients, alerts and retry logic all branch on errors. Inconsistent or leaky errors break integrations and expose internals to attackers.","prereqs":["03-apis-contracts.html#c1"],"practice":"Add RFC 7807 problem+json errors to a small API: validation (400), not found (404), conflict (409) and an unexpected 500. Make the 500 hide internals, carry a trace id, and keep the same code when the message text changes."},{"id":"03-apis-contracts.html#c6","page":"03-apis-contracts.html","pageTitle":"03 · APIs & contracts","title":"Rate limiting · token bucket","difficulty":3,"connects":["04-microservices-patterns.html#p2","03-apis-contracts.html#c5"],"references":[],"stage":"s3","level":"core","summary":"Limiting how fast each caller can send requests, most often with a token bucket, and answering 429 with Retry-After.","why":"One noisy client or script can exhaust capacity for everyone. Rate limits protect fairness, cost and downstream systems.","prereqs":["03-apis-contracts.html#c5"],"practice":"Implement a token bucket rate limiter in Redis with a Lua script, shared by two app instances. Load-test with k6 and confirm the limit holds across instances and returns correct Retry-After headers."},{"id":"03-apis-contracts.html#c7","page":"03-apis-contracts.html","pageTitle":"03 · APIs & contracts","title":"Authentication · the JWT flow","difficulty":4,"connects":["03-apis-contracts.html#c8"],"references":[],"stage":"s2","level":"core","summary":"Proving who the caller is: server sessions versus signed JWTs, and the short access token plus refresh token pattern.","why":"Authentication mistakes such as long-lived tokens, unverified signatures or tokens in local storage lead directly to account takeover. Token design also decides how you revoke access.","prereqs":["03-apis-contracts.html#c1"],"practice":"Build login with a 10-minute JWT access token and a refresh token stored server side. Show a protected call failing after expiry, a refresh succeeding, and revocation of the refresh token cutting off access."},{"id":"03-apis-contracts.html#c8","page":"03-apis-contracts.html","pageTitle":"03 · APIs & contracts","title":"Authorization · RBAC vs ABAC","difficulty":3,"connects":["03-apis-contracts.html#c7"],"references":[],"stage":"s2","level":"core","summary":"Deciding what an authenticated caller may do: role-based (RBAC) for coarse roles, attribute-based (ABAC) for rules such as row-level access.","why":"Broken access control is the top web security risk. A poor model either explodes into hundreds of roles or scatters checks through code where they are missed.","prereqs":["03-apis-contracts.html#c7"],"practice":"Model a multi-tenant documents API with RBAC roles (viewer, editor, admin) and an ABAC rule (same tenant and same department). Write the policy in OPA or Cedar and test ten allow and deny cases."},{"id":"03-apis-contracts.html#c9","page":"03-apis-contracts.html","pageTitle":"03 · APIs & contracts","title":"Idempotency keys","difficulty":3,"connects":["04-microservices-patterns.html#p1","03-apis-contracts.html#c5"],"references":[],"stage":"s2","level":"core","summary":"The detail of idempotency keys: a key moves through locked, completed and conflict states, stored with a request hash and the response.","why":"Naive key checks race under concurrent retries or accept a reused key with a different body. Getting the states right is what makes payment APIs safe.","prereqs":["04-microservices-patterns.html#p5","02-data-storage.html#c3"],"practice":"Implement the three key states with a Postgres unique constraint. Test: two identical requests at once (one waits or gets 409), a retry after success (cached response), and the same key with a different body (422)."},{"id":"03-apis-contracts.html#c10","page":"03-apis-contracts.html","pageTitle":"03 · APIs & contracts","title":"HTTP caching · ETag & 304","difficulty":2,"connects":["04-microservices-patterns.html#p4"],"references":[],"stage":"s3","level":"core","summary":"HTTP's own caching: Cache-Control for freshness and scope, ETag with If-None-Match for cheap 304 revalidation.","why":"Ignoring HTTP caching wastes bandwidth and server work; getting it wrong serves private data to the wrong user. It is the cheapest cache you already have.","prereqs":["03-apis-contracts.html#c1"],"practice":"Add Cache-Control and ETag headers to a REST resource. Use curl to show a 200, then a 304 on If-None-Match, then a 200 after an update, and mark a user-specific endpoint private."},{"id":"04-microservices-patterns.html#p1","page":"04-microservices-patterns.html","pageTitle":"04 · Microservices patterns","title":"Saga Pattern","difficulty":4,"connects":[],"references":[],"stage":"s5","level":"advanced","summary":"A long business transaction split into local transactions, each with a compensating action run in reverse if a later step fails.","why":"Distributed workflows cannot hold one ACID transaction, and without compensations a failure leaves money or stock stuck. Orchestration versus choreography is a common design question.","prereqs":["04-microservices-patterns.html#p6","02-data-storage.html#c3","04-microservices-patterns.html#p5"],"practice":"Implement a three-step booking saga (reserve seat, charge, send ticket) with an orchestrator that persists its state. Fail the third step and show compensations run in reverse, and that re-running a compensation is safe."},{"id":"04-microservices-patterns.html#p2","page":"04-microservices-patterns.html","pageTitle":"04 · Microservices patterns","title":"Circuit Breaker","difficulty":3,"connects":[],"references":[],"stage":"s3","level":"core","summary":"A guard that stops calling a failing dependency after a failure threshold, fails fast with a fallback, and probes for recovery.","why":"Without it a slow dependency ties up callers until they fail too, and the outage cascades. It also gives the failing service room to recover.","prereqs":["04-microservices-patterns.html#p3"],"practice":"Wrap a flaky downstream call in a circuit breaker (opossum in Node or pybreaker in Python). Inject a 10-second outage and log the closed, open and half-open transitions with latency before and after."},{"id":"04-microservices-patterns.html#p3","page":"04-microservices-patterns.html","pageTitle":"04 · Microservices patterns","title":"Retry with Backoff","difficulty":2,"connects":[],"references":[],"stage":"s3","level":"core","summary":"Retrying transient failures with exponentially growing, randomised (jittered) waits and a cap on attempts.","why":"Naive retries synchronise into a stampede that keeps a struggling service down. Retrying a non-idempotent call causes duplicates.","prereqs":["01-distributed-systems.html#c1","01-distributed-systems.html#c8"],"practice":"Simulate 200 clients hitting a service that fails 30% of calls. Compare fixed-interval retries, exponential backoff and full jitter, plotting request rate over time at the server."},{"id":"04-microservices-patterns.html#p4","page":"04-microservices-patterns.html","pageTitle":"04 · Microservices patterns","title":"API Gateway","difficulty":2,"connects":[],"references":[],"stage":"s5","level":"core","summary":"One entry point in front of many services that handles routing, auth, rate limiting and TLS, sometimes one per client type (BFF).","why":"A gateway centralises cross-cutting policy and hides internal topology. When business logic creeps into it, it becomes a new monolith and bottleneck.","prereqs":["03-apis-contracts.html#c1","03-apis-contracts.html#c7","03-apis-contracts.html#c6"],"practice":"Put Kong, Envoy or nginx in front of two small services. Configure path routing, JWT validation and a per-key rate limit at the gateway, and remove those checks from the services."},{"id":"04-microservices-patterns.html#p5","page":"04-microservices-patterns.html","pageTitle":"04 · Microservices patterns","title":"Idempotency","difficulty":2,"connects":[],"references":[],"stage":"s2","level":"core","summary":"The service pattern for idempotency: clients send an Idempotency-Key per logical attempt and the server replays the stored response on retry.","why":"POST endpoints with side effects double-charge or double-ship without it. It is a prerequisite for safe retries, at-least-once messaging and sagas.","prereqs":["01-distributed-systems.html#c8"],"practice":"Add an Idempotency-Key header to a POST /orders endpoint in a small service. Store key and response in Postgres, fire the same request ten times concurrently, and confirm exactly one order exists."},{"id":"04-microservices-patterns.html#p6","page":"04-microservices-patterns.html","pageTitle":"04 · Microservices patterns","title":"Event-Driven Architecture","difficulty":4,"connects":[],"references":[],"stage":"s5","level":"core","summary":"Services publish facts as events to a broker and others react, so producers do not call or know their consumers.","why":"Events decouple teams and absorb load, but they make flows harder to trace and bring eventual consistency and duplicate delivery. It is the backbone of most large backends.","prereqs":["04-microservices-patterns.html#p5","01-distributed-systems.html#c1"],"practice":"Build an OrderPlaced event on Kafka or Redpanda with two consumers (email and stock). Stop one consumer, place orders, restart it and show it catches up; then deliver a duplicate and make the consumer ignore it."},{"id":"04-microservices-patterns.html#p7","page":"04-microservices-patterns.html","pageTitle":"04 · Microservices patterns","title":"Bulkhead","difficulty":3,"connects":[],"references":[],"stage":"s3","level":"core","summary":"Separate, bounded resource pools (threads, connections, semaphores) per dependency so one slow dependency cannot drain them all.","why":"A single slow third party can block every worker and take down healthy features. Bulkheads keep a partial outage partial.","prereqs":["06-performance-scale.html#c5","04-microservices-patterns.html#p2"],"practice":"Build a service that calls two downstreams through one shared HTTP pool. Make one slow and show both fail, then give each its own bounded pool or semaphore and show the healthy one keeps working."},{"id":"04-microservices-patterns.html#p8","page":"04-microservices-patterns.html","pageTitle":"04 · Microservices patterns","title":"Service Discovery","difficulty":3,"connects":[],"references":[],"stage":"s4","level":"core","summary":"A registry where service instances register and send heartbeats so callers can find healthy instances by name.","why":"In dynamic environments addresses change all the time. Discovery and health checks decide whether traffic goes to dead instances.","prereqs":["01-distributed-systems.html#c10"],"practice":"Run Consul in dev mode with three instances of a small service registered with health checks. Kill one and show a client stops routing to it, measuring how long that takes."},{"id":"04-microservices-patterns.html#p9","page":"04-microservices-patterns.html","pageTitle":"04 · Microservices patterns","title":"CQRS · Command Query Responsibility Segregation","difficulty":5,"connects":[],"references":[],"stage":"s5","level":"advanced","summary":"Separate models for writes (validated, normalised) and reads (denormalised projections built from events).","why":"It lets reads and writes scale and change independently, at the cost of two models and eventual consistency. Used too early it adds complexity with no benefit.","prereqs":["04-microservices-patterns.html#p6","01-distributed-systems.html#c4"],"practice":"Build a write model for orders in Postgres that emits events, and a projector that builds a denormalised \"orders by customer\" view in another table or Elasticsearch. Drop the view and rebuild it by replaying events."},{"id":"04-microservices-patterns.html#p10","page":"04-microservices-patterns.html","pageTitle":"04 · Microservices patterns","title":"Transactional Outbox","difficulty":4,"connects":[],"references":[],"stage":"s5","level":"core","summary":"Writing the event to an outbox table in the same transaction as the business change, then relaying it to the broker.","why":"It is the standard fix for dual writes, giving at-least-once publishing from one ACID commit without two-phase commit.","prereqs":["02-data-storage.html#c10","01-distributed-systems.html#c9"],"practice":"Fix the dual-write exercise: write the order and an outbox row in one transaction, run a relay that polls and publishes, and kill it at random points. Show no events are lost and duplicates are handled by an idempotent consumer."},{"id":"04-microservices-patterns.html#p11","page":"04-microservices-patterns.html","pageTitle":"04 · Microservices patterns","title":"Sidecar","difficulty":2,"connects":[],"references":[],"stage":"s5","level":"core","summary":"A helper process deployed next to each service that handles networking concerns such as mTLS, retries and telemetry.","why":"Sidecars give one networking and security story across languages, at the cost of an extra hop and more configuration. They are the base of service meshes.","prereqs":["04-microservices-patterns.html#p2","04-microservices-patterns.html#p8"],"practice":"Run a small service with an Envoy sidecar in Docker Compose. Configure retries, a timeout and access logs in Envoy only, and show the app code calls plain localhost and gains those behaviours."},{"id":"04-microservices-patterns.html#p12","page":"04-microservices-patterns.html","pageTitle":"04 · Microservices patterns","title":"Strangler Fig","difficulty":3,"connects":[],"references":[],"stage":"s5","level":"core","summary":"Replacing a legacy system piece by piece behind a routing facade until the old system can be removed.","why":"Big-bang rewrites usually fail or run late. Incremental migration keeps shipping features and makes each step reversible.","prereqs":["04-microservices-patterns.html#p4"],"practice":"Put an nginx facade in front of a small \"legacy\" app. Move one route to a new service, keep the rest on the old app, add a feature flag to switch back, and write the order you would move the remaining routes."},{"id":"05-reliability-observability.html#c1","page":"05-reliability-observability.html","pageTitle":"05 · Reliability & observability","title":"SLI · SLO · error budgets","difficulty":2,"connects":["05-reliability-observability.html#c2","05-reliability-observability.html#c8","05-reliability-observability.html#c10"],"references":[],"stage":"s1","level":"foundation","summary":"An SLI measures service quality, an SLO sets the target, and the error budget (100% minus the SLO) is how much failure you can spend.","why":"SLOs turn \"reliable enough\" into a number that guides alerts, release pace and architecture spend. Without one, teams either over-build or argue after every outage.","prereqs":["05-reliability-observability.html#c2"],"practice":"Define an availability SLI and a 99.9% SLO for a sample API. Compute the monthly error budget, write a multi-window burn-rate alert in PromQL, and test it with synthetic error spikes."},{"id":"05-reliability-observability.html#c2","page":"05-reliability-observability.html","pageTitle":"05 · Reliability & observability","title":"The four golden signals","difficulty":2,"connects":["05-reliability-observability.html#c1","05-reliability-observability.html#c5","04-microservices-patterns.html#p2"],"references":[],"stage":"s1","level":"foundation","summary":"Latency, traffic, errors and saturation: the four measurements that show whether a service is healthy and where it is failing.","why":"Teams that watch only CPU or only averages miss outages and slow tails. These four signals are the base for SLOs, alerts and deploy checks.","prereqs":[],"practice":"Instrument a small service with Prometheus metrics for the four signals, with latency as a histogram split by success and error. Build a Grafana dashboard and trigger each signal with a scripted fault."},{"id":"05-reliability-observability.html#c3","page":"05-reliability-observability.html","pageTitle":"05 · Reliability & observability","title":"Structured logging & correlation IDs","difficulty":2,"connects":["05-reliability-observability.html#c4","05-reliability-observability.html#c5"],"references":[],"stage":"s1","level":"foundation","summary":"Logs written as key-value fields (JSON) and stamped with a correlation id so one request can be followed across services.","why":"Without structure and a shared id, an incident across several services becomes guesswork. It is also the base for audit logs and tracing.","prereqs":[],"practice":"Add JSON logging to two small services where service A calls B. Generate a request id at A, pass it in a header, bind it to every log line in both, and query one request end to end with jq."},{"id":"05-reliability-observability.html#c4","page":"05-reliability-observability.html","pageTitle":"05 · Reliability & observability","title":"Distributed tracing","difficulty":3,"connects":["05-reliability-observability.html#c3","05-reliability-observability.html#c5"],"references":[],"stage":"s3","level":"core","summary":"Recording each hop of a request as a timed span in one trace so you can see where latency and errors arise across services.","why":"Logs alone cannot show where time goes in a call that fans out across services. Tracing also needs a sampling decision that has cost implications.","prereqs":["05-reliability-observability.html#c3"],"practice":"Instrument three small services with OpenTelemetry auto-instrumentation and export to Jaeger. Add a slow database call in one service and find it from the trace waterfall."},{"id":"05-reliability-observability.html#c5","page":"05-reliability-observability.html","pageTitle":"05 · Reliability & observability","title":"Metrics cardinality","difficulty":3,"connects":["05-reliability-observability.html#c2","05-reliability-observability.html#c3","05-reliability-observability.html#c4"],"references":[],"stage":"s3","level":"core","summary":"Each unique label combination creates a new metric series, so labels must be bounded dimensions, not ids.","why":"A user id in a metric label can multiply series into the millions and break the monitoring system. High-cardinality data belongs in logs and traces.","prereqs":["05-reliability-observability.html#c2","05-reliability-observability.html#c3"],"practice":"Add a user_id label to a Prometheus counter and generate traffic for 50,000 users. Watch the series count and memory grow, then move user_id to logs and cap tenant labels to the top 10 plus \"other\"."},{"id":"05-reliability-observability.html#c6","page":"05-reliability-observability.html","pageTitle":"05 · Reliability & observability","title":"Graceful degradation","difficulty":3,"connects":["04-microservices-patterns.html#p2","05-reliability-observability.html#c7"],"references":[],"stage":"s3","level":"core","summary":"Designing so non-critical features fail or fall back to cached or empty results while the core path keeps working.","why":"Without it a failing extra, such as recommendations, takes down checkout. Architects must name the critical path and what may be dropped.","prereqs":["04-microservices-patterns.html#p2"],"practice":"Build a product page API that calls price, stock and recommendations. Kill recommendations and make the page still return price and stock with a fallback list, controlled by a feature flag."},{"id":"05-reliability-observability.html#c7","page":"05-reliability-observability.html","pageTitle":"05 · Reliability & observability","title":"Load shedding & backpressure","difficulty":4,"connects":["04-microservices-patterns.html#p2","03-apis-contracts.html#c6","05-reliability-observability.html#c6"],"references":[],"stage":"s3","level":"advanced","summary":"Rejecting excess work early (load shedding) and making slow consumers push back on producers (backpressure) instead of queuing without limit.","why":"Unbounded queues turn a traffic spike into a full outage with high latency for everyone. Shedding and backpressure keep the core path alive at capacity.","prereqs":["04-microservices-patterns.html#p7","03-apis-contracts.html#c6"],"practice":"Give a service a bounded queue and concurrency limit, and return 503 when it is full. Load past capacity and compare p99 latency and success rate with an unbounded version; then add a priority lane for health checks."},{"id":"05-reliability-observability.html#c8","page":"05-reliability-observability.html","pageTitle":"05 · Reliability & observability","title":"Deployment strategies","difficulty":3,"connects":["05-reliability-observability.html#c1","05-reliability-observability.html#c9","05-reliability-observability.html#c2"],"references":[],"stage":"s3","level":"core","summary":"Rolling, blue/green and canary releases, and automatic promotion or rollback based on live metrics.","why":"Deploys are the biggest source of incidents. The strategy decides how many users a bad release reaches and how fast you can undo it.","prereqs":["05-reliability-observability.html#c1","05-reliability-observability.html#c2"],"practice":"Run two versions of a small service behind nginx or Envoy with weighted routing. Send 5% to a version with a planted error, detect it from the error rate with a script, and shift traffic back automatically."},{"id":"05-reliability-observability.html#c9","page":"05-reliability-observability.html","pageTitle":"05 · Reliability & observability","title":"Zero-downtime migrations","difficulty":4,"connects":["05-reliability-observability.html#c8","03-apis-contracts.html#c3"],"references":[],"stage":"s5","level":"advanced","summary":"Changing a database schema in expand, migrate, switch reads and contract steps so old and new app versions both keep working.","why":"A single-step rename or type change breaks whichever app version is still running during a deploy. Every live system needs this discipline.","prereqs":["05-reliability-observability.html#c8","03-apis-contracts.html#c3"],"practice":"Rename a column in a running app with no downtime: add the new column, dual-write, backfill in batches, switch reads behind a flag, then drop the old column. Run a load test throughout and confirm zero errors."},{"id":"05-reliability-observability.html#c10","page":"05-reliability-observability.html","pageTitle":"05 · Reliability & observability","title":"Incident response & postmortems","difficulty":2,"connects":["05-reliability-observability.html#c1","05-reliability-observability.html#c8","04-microservices-patterns.html#p2"],"references":[],"stage":"s3","level":"core","summary":"Running incidents with clear roles (an incident commander who coordinates), mitigating before debugging, and writing blameless postmortems.","why":"Unstructured incidents last longer and teach nothing. Postmortems with tracked actions are how systems and teams actually improve.","prereqs":["05-reliability-observability.html#c1","05-reliability-observability.html#c2"],"practice":"Take a published public postmortem (for example a cloud provider outage report). Rewrite it in a blameless template with timeline, detection, mitigation, root causes and three tracked actions."},{"id":"06-performance-scale.html#c1","page":"06-performance-scale.html","pageTitle":"06 · Performance & scale","title":"Latency numbers every engineer should know","difficulty":1,"connects":["06-performance-scale.html#c4","06-performance-scale.html#c5","06-performance-scale.html#c8"],"references":[],"stage":"s1","level":"foundation","summary":"Rough costs of memory, disk, network and cross-region calls, used to estimate where time goes before you measure.","why":"Without these numbers you cannot judge whether a cache, a network hop or a second region is worth it. Back-of-envelope estimates in design reviews and interviews depend on them.","prereqs":[],"practice":"Time a local in-memory map lookup, a Redis GET on localhost, a Postgres primary-key SELECT and an HTTPS call to a public API, 1,000 times each. Put p50 and p99 in a table next to the published numbers and explain the gaps."},{"id":"06-performance-scale.html#c2","page":"06-performance-scale.html","pageTitle":"06 · Performance & scale","title":"Profiling & flame graphs","difficulty":3,"connects":["06-performance-scale.html#c1","06-performance-scale.html#c3","05-reliability-observability.html#c4"],"references":[],"stage":"s3","level":"core","summary":"Sampling stack traces and drawing them as flame graphs to find where CPU or waiting time goes.","why":"Optimising without a profile usually targets the wrong code. On-CPU and off-CPU profiles answer different questions.","prereqs":["06-performance-scale.html#c6"],"practice":"Write a small service with a deliberate hot loop and a slow I/O call. Profile it with py-spy or node --cpu-prof, generate a flame graph, find both problems and fix the bigger one."},{"id":"06-performance-scale.html#c3","page":"06-performance-scale.html","pageTitle":"06 · Performance & scale","title":"The N+1 query problem","difficulty":2,"connects":["02-data-storage.html#c1","03-apis-contracts.html#c4"],"references":[],"stage":"s1","level":"foundation","summary":"A loop that runs one query per item (1 + N round trips) instead of one JOIN or batched query, often hidden by ORM lazy loading.","why":"N+1 is the most common reason an endpoint is slow only in production data volumes. It multiplies database load as traffic grows.","prereqs":["02-data-storage.html#c2","06-performance-scale.html#c1"],"practice":"Build an endpoint that lists 100 orders with their customer through an ORM and log the query count. Fix it with a JOIN, then with a batched IN query, and compare latency and query counts."},{"id":"06-performance-scale.html#c4","page":"06-performance-scale.html","pageTitle":"06 · Performance & scale","title":"Cache strategies","difficulty":3,"connects":["06-performance-scale.html#c1","03-apis-contracts.html#c10","06-performance-scale.html#c8"],"references":[],"stage":"s3","level":"core","summary":"Ways to use a cache (cache-aside, write-through, write-behind), how to invalidate it, and how to stop stampedes on expiry.","why":"Caches are the most common performance fix and a common source of stale or lost data. Invalidation and stampede bugs often cause outages.","prereqs":["06-performance-scale.html#c1"],"practice":"Put Redis cache-aside in front of a slow query. Measure hit rate and latency, then expire a hot key under 500 concurrent requests to cause a stampede, and fix it with a lock or early refresh."},{"id":"06-performance-scale.html#c5","page":"06-performance-scale.html","pageTitle":"06 · Performance & scale","title":"Connection pooling","difficulty":2,"connects":["06-performance-scale.html#c1","05-reliability-observability.html#c2"],"references":[],"stage":"s1","level":"foundation","summary":"Reusing a fixed set of open database connections instead of opening a new one per request, with a sized limit.","why":"Wrong pool sizes cause either queuing in the app or an overloaded database. In multi-instance deployments the total connection count can exhaust the database.","prereqs":["06-performance-scale.html#c1"],"practice":"Run a load test against an endpoint that opens a new Postgres connection per request, then with a pool of 5, 20 and 200. Plot latency and database CPU for each and explain the best size."},{"id":"06-performance-scale.html#c6","page":"06-performance-scale.html","pageTitle":"06 · Performance & scale","title":"Async vs sync I/O","difficulty":3,"connects":["06-performance-scale.html#c5","06-performance-scale.html#c7","05-reliability-observability.html#c7"],"references":[],"stage":"s1","level":"core","summary":"Blocking I/O holds a thread while waiting; non-blocking I/O lets one thread serve many requests by switching on readiness.","why":"The concurrency model sets how many requests a service can hold and how it fails under slow dependencies. A CPU-heavy task on an event loop stalls every request.","prereqs":["06-performance-scale.html#c1"],"practice":"Write the same endpoint that calls a 200 ms upstream in sync Python (Flask) and async Python (FastAPI with httpx). Load each with 500 concurrent clients, then add a CPU-heavy step to the async one and watch latency collapse."},{"id":"06-performance-scale.html#c7","page":"06-performance-scale.html","pageTitle":"06 · Performance & scale","title":"Batching & streaming","difficulty":2,"connects":["04-microservices-patterns.html#p6","05-reliability-observability.html#c7"],"references":[],"stage":"s3","level":"core","summary":"Grouping many items into one call to spread fixed overhead (batching), or sending items continuously through a stream.","why":"Item-by-item calls waste most of their time on overhead. The batch size and flush interval trade latency for throughput.","prereqs":["06-performance-scale.html#c1"],"practice":"Insert 1 million rows into Postgres one at a time, with batched multi-row INSERTs of 100 and 1,000, and with COPY. Chart throughput and explain where the gains flatten out."},{"id":"06-performance-scale.html#c8","page":"06-performance-scale.html","pageTitle":"06 · Performance & scale","title":"CDN & edge caching","difficulty":2,"connects":["03-apis-contracts.html#c10","06-performance-scale.html#c4"],"references":[],"stage":"s3","level":"core","summary":"A network of edge caches near users that serve static assets and cacheable API responses without touching the origin.","why":"A CDN cuts latency and origin load sharply for static and public content. Caching a personalised response at the edge leaks data across users.","prereqs":["03-apis-contracts.html#c10"],"practice":"Put a free-tier CDN (or a local Varnish or nginx cache) in front of a small app. Serve static files with content-hashed names and a public list endpoint with s-maxage and stale-while-revalidate, and check the cache headers."},{"id":"06-performance-scale.html#c9","page":"06-performance-scale.html","pageTitle":"06 · Performance & scale","title":"Hot keys & shard skew","difficulty":4,"connects":["06-performance-scale.html#c4"],"references":[],"stage":"s4","level":"advanced","summary":"A few very popular keys send most traffic to one shard or cache node, which saturates while others are idle.","why":"Even sharding fails under real skewed traffic such as a viral product. Detecting and splitting hot keys keeps one node from limiting the whole system.","prereqs":["02-data-storage.html#c7","06-performance-scale.html#c4"],"practice":"Generate Zipf-distributed traffic over a sharded key-value store (simulated or Redis Cluster). Measure per-shard load, then apply key salting and a local in-process cache and compare the skew."},{"id":"06-performance-scale.html#c10","page":"06-performance-scale.html","pageTitle":"06 · Performance & scale","title":"Load testing","difficulty":3,"connects":["05-reliability-observability.html#c1","05-reliability-observability.html#c7"],"references":[],"stage":"s3","level":"core","summary":"Ramping traffic in a controlled test to find the knee (latency starts to climb) and the cliff (errors spike) of a system.","why":"Without a known capacity the first real spike finds the limit in production. Release-to-release tests catch performance regressions.","prereqs":["05-reliability-observability.html#c1","05-reliability-observability.html#c2"],"practice":"Write a k6 script that ramps a small API from 10 to 2,000 requests per second. Plot p50 and p99 against load, mark the knee and cliff, and state a safe capacity at 70% of the knee."},{"id":"07-security.html#c1","page":"07-security.html","pageTitle":"07 · Security","title":"Defence in depth","difficulty":2,"connects":["07-security.html#c5","07-security.html#c9","07-security.html#c10"],"references":[],"stage":"s1","level":"foundation","summary":"Security built as layers (edge, network, application, data) where each layer assumes the one outside it has been breached.","why":"A single wall fails completely when it is bypassed. Layering limits the damage of any one mistake and frames every later security decision.","prereqs":[],"practice":"Draw a three-tier web app and list one control at each layer (edge, network, app, data). For each, write the attack it stops and what happens if only that layer exists."},{"id":"07-security.html#c2","page":"07-security.html","pageTitle":"07 · Security","title":"Threat modelling (STRIDE)","difficulty":3,"connects":["07-security.html#c1","07-security.html#c4","03-apis-contracts.html#c7"],"references":[],"stage":"s6","level":"core","summary":"A structured threat review using six categories (spoofing, tampering, repudiation, information disclosure, denial of service, elevation of privilege).","why":"Threats found at design time are cheap to fix; found after release they are incidents. Architects are expected to run this in design reviews.","prereqs":["07-security.html#c1","03-apis-contracts.html#c7"],"practice":"Draw a data-flow diagram with trust boundaries for a small file-sharing app. Apply STRIDE to each flow, rank the top five threats by impact and likelihood, and name a mitigation or explicit acceptance for each."},{"id":"07-security.html#c3","page":"07-security.html","pageTitle":"07 · Security","title":"Secrets management","difficulty":3,"connects":["07-security.html#c4","07-security.html#c6","07-security.html#c8"],"references":[],"stage":"s2","level":"core","summary":"Keeping credentials out of code and images: a secrets manager, workload identity to fetch them, and short-lived rotating secrets.","why":"Leaked secrets in repos and images are a leading cause of breaches. Rotation and short lifetimes limit how long a leak is useful.","prereqs":["07-security.html#c1"],"practice":"Run HashiCorp Vault in dev mode, enable the database secrets engine for Postgres, and have a small app fetch 1-hour credentials at start-up. Add gitleaks as a pre-commit hook and show it blocks a planted key."},{"id":"07-security.html#c4","page":"07-security.html","pageTitle":"07 · Security","title":"Least privilege & IAM","difficulty":3,"connects":["07-security.html#c3","03-apis-contracts.html#c8","07-security.html#c10"],"references":[],"stage":"s2","level":"core","summary":"Giving every user, service and token only the permissions it needs, so a compromise has a bounded blast radius.","why":"Broad permissions turn one compromised service into a full breach. Least privilege is also what auditors check first.","prereqs":["03-apis-contracts.html#c8","07-security.html#c1"],"practice":"Create a Postgres role for an app that can SELECT and INSERT on two tables only, and a cloud IAM policy (or MinIO policy) limited to one bucket prefix. Prove that DROP, DELETE elsewhere and other prefixes are denied."},{"id":"07-security.html#c5","page":"07-security.html","pageTitle":"07 · Security","title":"mTLS & service identity","difficulty":4,"connects":["07-security.html#c1","07-security.html#c6","07-security.html#c10"],"references":[],"stage":"s6","level":"advanced","summary":"Mutual TLS: both client and server present certificates, giving each workload a verifiable identity.","why":"Network location is not identity; mTLS stops a rogue workload from impersonating a service. Certificate issuance and rotation are the hard operational part.","prereqs":["07-security.html#c6","03-apis-contracts.html#c7"],"practice":"Create a small CA with step-ca or openssl, issue certificates to two services, and configure mTLS between them. Show a client without a valid certificate is rejected, then rotate a certificate without downtime."},{"id":"07-security.html#c6","page":"07-security.html","pageTitle":"07 · Security","title":"Encryption at rest & in transit","difficulty":3,"connects":["07-security.html#c3","07-security.html#c5"],"references":[],"stage":"s2","level":"core","summary":"TLS for data moving between systems and disk, object or column encryption for stored data, with keys held in a KMS.","why":"Unencrypted traffic or backups expose data to anyone on the path or with storage access. Key management decides who can actually read it.","prereqs":["07-security.html#c3"],"practice":"Encrypt a PII column with envelope encryption: generate a data key from a local KMS (Vault Transit), encrypt values, store only ciphertext, and rotate the key-encryption key without re-encrypting all rows."},{"id":"07-security.html#c7","page":"07-security.html","pageTitle":"07 · Security","title":"Supply-chain security & SBOM","difficulty":3,"connects":["07-security.html#c3","07-security.html#c8"],"references":[],"stage":"s6","level":"core","summary":"Securing everything you build from: dependency scanning, a software bill of materials (SBOM), and signed artifacts.","why":"Most code you ship is third-party, and attacks increasingly target dependencies and build pipelines. You cannot respond to a new CVE without knowing what you run.","prereqs":["07-security.html#c1"],"practice":"Generate an SBOM for a small container image with syft, scan it with grype, upgrade one vulnerable dependency, and sign the image with cosign, then verify the signature."},{"id":"07-security.html#c8","page":"07-security.html","pageTitle":"07 · Security","title":"Audit logging","difficulty":2,"connects":["05-reliability-observability.html#c3","07-security.html#c2"],"references":[],"stage":"s6","level":"core","summary":"An append-only, tamper-evident record of who did what, when and from where, kept where even admins cannot change it.","why":"After a breach or a dispute, the audit trail is the evidence. Regulators and finance require it, and it must be designed in, not added later.","prereqs":["05-reliability-observability.html#c3","07-security.html#c4"],"practice":"Build an append-only audit table where each row stores a hash of the previous row. Write a verifier that detects an edited or deleted row, and record login, role-change and data-export events from a sample app."},{"id":"07-security.html#c9","page":"07-security.html","pageTitle":"07 · Security","title":"Rate limiting & DDoS defence","difficulty":3,"connects":["03-apis-contracts.html#c6","05-reliability-observability.html#c7"],"references":[],"stage":"s6","level":"core","summary":"Layered defence against floods and abuse: edge DDoS protection, a WAF for application attacks, and per-client rate limits in the app.","why":"No single layer can absorb a large attack. Architects must decide which layer handles which traffic and what the service does when limits are hit.","prereqs":["03-apis-contracts.html#c6","05-reliability-observability.html#c7","07-security.html#c1"],"practice":"Write a short layered plan for a public API: what the edge, the WAF and the app each block. Then run a local nginx with limit_req and a simple WAF rule (ModSecurity CRS) and test both with a load script and a SQL injection probe."},{"id":"07-security.html#c10","page":"07-security.html","pageTitle":"07 · Security","title":"Zero trust","difficulty":5,"connects":["07-security.html#c1","07-security.html#c5","07-security.html#c4"],"references":[],"stage":"s6","level":"advanced","summary":"No implicit trust based on network location: every call carries a verified identity and is authorised by policy every time.","why":"Perimeter security fails once anything inside is compromised. Zero trust is a target architecture that changes identity, networking and authorisation together.","prereqs":["07-security.html#c5","07-security.html#c4","03-apis-contracts.html#c8"],"practice":"Extend the mTLS exercise: add OPA as a policy check so service A may call only GET /orders on service B. Show a valid certificate with the wrong identity or path is denied, and log every decision."},{"id":"08-data-platforms.html#c1","page":"08-data-platforms.html","pageTitle":"08 · Data platform architecture","title":"OLTP vs OLAP · why analytics gets its own platform","difficulty":2,"connects":["08-data-platforms.html#c2","08-data-platforms.html#c4","02-data-storage.html#c6","04-microservices-patterns.html#p9"],"references":[{"url":"https://duckdb.org/docs/","title":"DuckDB documentation"}],"stage":"s6","level":"foundation","summary":"Transaction systems (OLTP) read and write a few rows by key; analytics (OLAP) scans and aggregates history, so it needs its own platform.","why":"Running reports on the production database hurts transactions and cannot join sources or keep history. Deciding when a replica is enough and when a platform is needed is an architect call.","prereqs":["02-data-storage.html#c1","02-data-storage.html#c5"],"practice":"Seed a Postgres OLTP schema with 10 million order lines. Run a monthly revenue aggregate while a load test runs order inserts, and measure the impact on insert latency. Then run the same aggregate in DuckDB over an export."},{"id":"08-data-platforms.html#c2","page":"08-data-platforms.html","pageTitle":"08 · Data platform architecture","title":"Columnar storage and Parquet","difficulty":3,"connects":["08-data-platforms.html#c3","08-data-platforms.html#c5","02-data-storage.html#c1","06-performance-scale.html#c7"],"references":[{"url":"https://parquet.apache.org/docs/","title":"Apache Parquet documentation"}],"stage":"s6","level":"core","summary":"Parquet stores each column together in compressed row groups with min and max statistics so queries read only needed columns and skip rows.","why":"Columnar layout is why analytics engines are fast and cheap. Clustering and file size decide whether that pruning actually works.","prereqs":["08-data-platforms.html#c1","02-data-storage.html#c1"],"practice":"Export 20 million rows to CSV and to Parquet (unsorted and sorted by date). With DuckDB, compare file sizes and the time of a one-month, three-column query on each, and inspect the row-group statistics."},{"id":"08-data-platforms.html#c3","page":"08-data-platforms.html","pageTitle":"08 · Data platform architecture","title":"Object storage as the lake","difficulty":2,"connects":["08-data-platforms.html#c2","08-data-platforms.html#c5","08-data-platforms.html#c6","07-security.html#c6"],"references":[{"url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/storage/blobs/lifecycle-management-overview","title":"Azure Blob Storage lifecycle management"}],"stage":"s6","level":"core","summary":"Object stores (S3, Azure Blob, ADLS) as the durable lake: flat keys, whole-object writes, no atomic rename, with conditional writes and versioning.","why":"Treating object storage like a file system leads to partial reads and broken commits. Its limits shape every lakehouse design.","prereqs":["08-data-platforms.html#c1"],"practice":"Run MinIO locally. Upload versioned objects, try a \"directory rename\" and count the operations, and use a conditional write (If-None-Match) so two writers cannot both create the same key."},{"id":"08-data-platforms.html#c4","page":"08-data-platforms.html","pageTitle":"08 · Data platform architecture","title":"Lake, warehouse, lakehouse · medallion layers as contracts","difficulty":2,"connects":["08-data-platforms.html#c5","08-data-platforms.html#c7","08-data-platforms.html#c9","07-security.html#c4"],"references":[],"stage":"s6","level":"core","summary":"Warehouse, lake and lakehouse as storage architectures, and bronze, silver and gold layers as contracts about what each layer guarantees.","why":"Without clear layer contracts, dashboards read raw data and nobody can rebuild after a logic fix. The storage choice decides lock-in, cost and who can read the data.","prereqs":["08-data-platforms.html#c1","08-data-platforms.html#c3"],"practice":"Design bronze, silver and gold layers for two sample sources (orders and shipments). For each layer write the one-paragraph contract, who may read it, and a CI rule that stops gold reading bronze."},{"id":"08-data-platforms.html#c5","page":"08-data-platforms.html","pageTitle":"08 · Data platform architecture","title":"Table formats · ACID on files","difficulty":4,"connects":["08-data-platforms.html#c2","08-data-platforms.html#c3","08-data-platforms.html#c8","02-data-storage.html#c3","02-data-storage.html#c5"],"references":[{"url":"https://iceberg.apache.org/spec/","title":"Apache Iceberg table spec"}],"stage":"s6","level":"advanced","summary":"Iceberg, Delta and DuckLake add a metadata layer over Parquet files to give atomic commits, MERGE, time travel and schema evolution.","why":"A folder of files has no transactions, so failed jobs and concurrent writers corrupt it. Table formats bring database guarantees to the lake, with compaction and housekeeping as the cost.","prereqs":["08-data-platforms.html#c2","08-data-platforms.html#c4","02-data-storage.html#c5"],"practice":"Create an Iceberg or Delta table locally (PySpark or DuckDB with the delta extension). Append, MERGE corrections, then query an earlier snapshot. Run two concurrent writers and observe the optimistic commit conflict and retry."},{"id":"08-data-platforms.html#c6","page":"08-data-platforms.html","pageTitle":"08 · Data platform architecture","title":"Ingestion · full, incremental, CDC, push vs pull","difficulty":3,"connects":["02-data-storage.html#c8","01-distributed-systems.html#c8","02-data-storage.html#c10","04-microservices-patterns.html#p10","07-security.html#c3"],"references":[],"stage":"s6","level":"core","summary":"Getting data in by full extract, incremental watermark or CDC, and choosing push or pull based on who can open the connection.","why":"Each extract method misses something (deletes, late commits, or cost at scale). Idempotent landing and correct watermarks decide whether numbers are complete.","prereqs":["02-data-storage.html#c8","01-distributed-systems.html#c8","08-data-platforms.html#c3"],"practice":"Build an incremental extract from Postgres using updated_at with a lookback window. Insert a row committed late by a long transaction and a hard delete, show which ones the extract misses, then land batches idempotently and MERGE by key."},{"id":"08-data-platforms.html#c7","page":"08-data-platforms.html","pageTitle":"08 · Data platform architecture","title":"Dimensional modelling · grain, facts, conformed dimensions, SCD2","difficulty":3,"connects":["08-data-platforms.html#c4","08-data-platforms.html#c9","08-data-platforms.html#c10","03-apis-contracts.html#c3"],"references":[{"url":"https://docs.getdbt.com/docs/build/snapshots","title":"dbt snapshots"}],"stage":"s6","level":"core","summary":"Modelling analytics as facts at a declared grain plus shared (conformed) dimensions, with SCD type 2 to keep attribute history.","why":"Without a clear grain, totals double count; without conformed dimensions, sources cannot be compared. SCD2 is how history reports as it was.","prereqs":["08-data-platforms.html#c4"],"practice":"From a sample orders dataset, declare the grain for a sales fact, build a date, product and customer dimension, and implement SCD type 2 for customer region in SQL. Show last year's sales stay in the old region after a change."},{"id":"08-data-platforms.html#c8","page":"08-data-platforms.html","pageTitle":"08 · Data platform architecture","title":"Orchestration · assets, partitions, backfills","difficulty":3,"connects":["08-data-platforms.html#c6","08-data-platforms.html#c9","01-distributed-systems.html#c8","04-microservices-patterns.html#p3"],"references":[{"url":"https://docs.dagster.io/","title":"Dagster documentation"}],"stage":"s6","level":"core","summary":"Running pipelines as a graph of data assets with partitions, so each run replaces one addressable slice and backfills are safe.","why":"Task-only schedulers know a job exited zero, not whether data is current. Append-style runs double numbers on retry; partition overwrite makes retries and backfills safe.","prereqs":["08-data-platforms.html#c6","01-distributed-systems.html#c8"],"practice":"Build a small Dagster (or dbt plus a script) pipeline with daily partitions that overwrite themselves. Re-run one day twice and prove the totals do not change, then backfill a week with bounded concurrency."},{"id":"08-data-platforms.html#c9","page":"08-data-platforms.html","pageTitle":"08 · Data platform architecture","title":"Correctness · reconciliation, contracts, freshness","difficulty":3,"connects":["08-data-platforms.html#c6","08-data-platforms.html#c10","05-reliability-observability.html#c1","03-apis-contracts.html#c3"],"references":[{"url":"https://docs.getdbt.com/docs/build/sources","title":"dbt sources and freshness"}],"stage":"s6","level":"core","summary":"Proving data is right with reconciliation against source control totals, data contracts with producers, and freshness SLOs.","why":"Shape tests pass while numbers are wrong. Finance trusts a platform only when it proves totals match the source on every load.","prereqs":["08-data-platforms.html#c6","05-reliability-observability.html#c1","03-apis-contracts.html#c3"],"practice":"Build a pipeline that loads orders and also extracts source-side counts and sums per day. Write a reconciliation check that blocks publishing on any difference, plant a missing row, and confirm it is caught and named."},{"id":"08-data-platforms.html#c10","page":"08-data-platforms.html","pageTitle":"08 · Data platform architecture","title":"Serving · marts, semantic layer, atomic publish, build vs buy","difficulty":4,"connects":["08-data-platforms.html#c4","08-data-platforms.html#c7","08-data-platforms.html#c9","06-performance-scale.html#c4","07-security.html#c4"],"references":[{"url":"https://docs.getdbt.com/docs/build/semantic-models","title":"dbt semantic models"}],"stage":"s6","level":"advanced","summary":"Serving data as marts and a semantic layer that defines each metric once, published atomically, and choosing build versus buy.","why":"Metric logic copied into every report drifts apart, and half-published data misleads. The serving and build-or-buy choices fix cost and team load for years.","prereqs":["08-data-platforms.html#c7","08-data-platforms.html#c9","07-security.html#c4"],"practice":"Define revenue and gross margin once in a dbt semantic layer (or a SQL view layer). Build into a staging schema, reconcile, then swap it in with one transactional rename, and show a reader never sees a half-built version."},{"id":"08-data-platforms.html#c11","page":"08-data-platforms.html","pageTitle":"08 · Data platform architecture","title":"Stream processing · event time, windows, watermarks, state","difficulty":4,"connects":["08-data-platforms.html#c6","01-distributed-systems.html#c9","04-microservices-patterns.html#p6","05-reliability-observability.html#c7","08-data-platforms.html#c9"],"references":[{"url":"https://nightlies.apache.org/flink/flink-docs-stable/docs/concepts/time/","title":"Flink: timely stream processing"}],"stage":"s6","level":"advanced","summary":"Computing continuously over an event log: event time vs processing time, tumbling, sliding and session windows, watermarks for late data, and checkpointed state.","why":"Some decisions cannot wait for a nightly batch, and streams that window by arrival time or drop late events give confidently wrong answers. Choosing stream, micro-batch or batch from the freshness SLO is an architect call.","prereqs":["08-data-platforms.html#c6","01-distributed-systems.html#c9","04-microservices-patterns.html#p6"],"practice":"Run Kafka (or Redpanda) and Flink SQL locally. Produce temperature readings with event timestamps, including a burst of 20-minute-old readings from a reconnecting truck. Compare processing-time and event-time tumbling windows, add a 2-minute watermark, and count readings that arrive after their window closed. Kill the job mid-window and show checkpoints restore state, with an idempotent sink so no alert is sent twice."},{"id":"08-data-platforms.html#c12","page":"08-data-platforms.html","pageTitle":"08 · Data platform architecture","title":"Data observability · volume, schema, distribution, lineage","difficulty":3,"connects":["08-data-platforms.html#c9","08-data-platforms.html#c8","05-reliability-observability.html#c2","05-reliability-observability.html#c1","03-apis-contracts.html#c3"],"references":[{"url":"https://docs.elementary-data.com/data-tests/anomaly-detection-tests/volume-anomalies","title":"elementary: volume anomalies"}],"stage":"s6","level":"core","summary":"Watching every table for freshness, volume, schema, distribution and lineage, with seasonal baselines, owners and blast radius on each alert.","why":"Pipelines run green while data goes wrong: a renamed column, a half-empty load, a null spike. Observability catches the failures nobody wrote a test for, and lineage says who is affected.","prereqs":["08-data-platforms.html#c9","08-data-platforms.html#c8","05-reliability-observability.html#c2"],"practice":"Simulate three weeks of daily loads into a metrics table (row count, null rate per column, schema hash). Plant a 40% drop on a Monday, a renamed column and a null spike, detect each against a same-weekday baseline, and print the downstream impact from a dbt manifest.json."},{"id":"08-data-platforms.html#c13","page":"08-data-platforms.html","pageTitle":"08 · Data platform architecture","title":"Governance · classification, masking, ownership, access audit","difficulty":4,"connects":["07-security.html#c4","03-apis-contracts.html#c8","07-security.html#c8","08-data-platforms.html#c5","08-data-platforms.html#c10","lakehouse-platform.html#c7"],"references":[{"url":"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/ddl-rowsecurity.html","title":"PostgreSQL row security policies"}],"stage":"s6","level":"advanced","summary":"Classifying columns, attaching row-level security and masking policies to labels, recording ownership in a catalog, and auditing every read of governed data.","why":"Personal data on a shared platform carries legal duties (POPIA, GDPR). Policies tied to labels scale where per-table grants do not, and engine-level controls fail if the storage underneath is open.","prereqs":["07-security.html#c4","03-apis-contracts.html#c8","07-security.html#c8","08-data-platforms.html#c10"],"practice":"In Postgres, label two personal columns, add row-level security by entity driven by a session setting (with FORCE so the owner is bound too), and a masked view for analysts. Prove an analyst sees masked, scoped rows and HR sees all. Then delete a person from an Iceberg or Delta table and show they remain in an old snapshot until it expires."},{"id":"payment-system.html#c1","page":"payment-system.html","pageTitle":"Applied A · Payment processing","title":"High-level architecture & payment lifecycle","difficulty":4,"connects":["04-microservices-patterns.html#p5","07-security.html#c3"],"references":[{"url":"https://stripe.com/blog/payment-api-design","title":"Stripe — Designing a Payment API"}],"stage":"s7","level":"core","summary":"The payment lifecycle (intent, authorise, capture, settle, payout) and the services around it: API, ledger, PSP adapter, webhook worker.","why":"Treating a payment as one API call loses the authorise and capture split, refunds and settlement. It is a standard interview design and a test of correctness thinking.","prereqs":["03-apis-contracts.html#c9","04-microservices-patterns.html#p6","02-data-storage.html#c3"],"practice":"Draw the payment architecture from memory in 30 minutes, then build a stub PSP adapter with authorise and capture calls and an API that creates payment intents with idempotency keys."},{"id":"payment-system.html#c2","page":"payment-system.html","pageTitle":"Applied A · Payment processing","title":"Double-entry ledger","difficulty":4,"connects":["07-security.html#c8","01-distributed-systems.html#c6"],"references":[{"url":"https://stripe.com/blog/accounting-for-developers","title":"Stripe — Accounting for developers"},{"url":"https://developer.squareup.com/blog/books-an-immutable-double-entry-accounting-database-service","title":"Square — Books: immutable double-entry at scale"}],"stage":"s7","level":"core","summary":"Recording every money movement as balanced debit and credit entries in an append-only ledger, with balances computed from entries.","why":"A mutable balance field loses history and drifts under races. Double entry gives an audit trail, simple refunds and a built-in correctness check.","prereqs":["payment-system.html#c1","02-data-storage.html#c4","07-security.html#c8"],"practice":"Build a double-entry ledger in Postgres with integer cents: payment, fee and refund postings, a constraint or check that each transaction sums to zero, and a balance query. Run concurrent postings and confirm the books still balance."},{"id":"payment-system.html#c3","page":"payment-system.html","pageTitle":"Applied A · Payment processing","title":"Idempotency & exactly-once processing","difficulty":3,"connects":["03-apis-contracts.html#c9","payment-system.html#c4"],"references":[{"url":"https://stripe.com/blog/idempotency","title":"Stripe — Designing robust idempotency keys"}],"stage":"s7","level":"core","summary":"Storing the idempotency key, request hash and response in the same transaction as the payment so a retry never charges twice.","why":"The double charge is the classic payment failure. Where and how the key is stored decides whether concurrent retries are safe.","prereqs":["payment-system.html#c1","03-apis-contracts.html#c9","01-distributed-systems.html#c9"],"practice":"Add idempotency to the payment API with the key stored in the same transaction as the payment row. Fire concurrent retries with a slow fake PSP, and confirm one PSP call, a 409 while in flight, and the cached result after."},{"id":"payment-system.html#c4","page":"payment-system.html","pageTitle":"Applied A · Payment processing","title":"Payment state machine","difficulty":3,"connects":["payment-system.html#c2","payment-system.html#c5","04-microservices-patterns.html#p6"],"references":[{"url":"https://gocardless.com/blog/state-machines","title":"GoCardless — Statesman: a state machine approach"}],"stage":"s7","level":"core","summary":"Payment states and allowed transitions enforced by one transition function, with each transition stored and emitted as an event.","why":"Free-form status strings allow impossible jumps such as pending to settled. One transition function keeps the business rules in one tested place.","prereqs":["payment-system.html#c1","02-data-storage.html#c4"],"practice":"Implement a payment state machine as a pure function (state, event) to (new state, effects), persist transitions in a transaction with a version check, and write table-driven tests for every legal and illegal transition."},{"id":"payment-system.html#c5","page":"payment-system.html","pageTitle":"Applied A · Payment processing","title":"Saga: multi-party payment flow","difficulty":5,"connects":["04-microservices-patterns.html#p5","payment-system.html#c3","payment-system.html#c4"],"references":[{"url":"https://eng.uber.com/cadence/","title":"Uber — Cadence: fault-tolerant orchestration"}],"stage":"s7","level":"advanced","summary":"An orchestrated saga for checkout (reserve stock, charge, ship) where each step has a defined compensation and the saga state is persisted.","why":"Without compensations, a failure mid-checkout leaves money or stock stuck. Choosing orchestration for money flows is a key design judgement.","prereqs":["payment-system.html#c3","payment-system.html#c4","04-microservices-patterns.html#p1"],"practice":"Build the checkout saga with Temporal (local dev server) or a persisted state table. Fail payment after reservation and shipping after payment, and verify compensations restore stock and refund exactly once."},{"id":"payment-system.html#c6","page":"payment-system.html","pageTitle":"Applied A · Payment processing","title":"Tokenisation & PCI compliance","difficulty":3,"connects":["07-security.html#c6","07-security.html#c3"],"references":[{"url":"https://stripe.com/docs/security","title":"Stripe — Security at Stripe"},{"url":"https://www.adyen.com/knowledge-hub/network-tokenization","title":"Adyen — Network tokenisation"}],"stage":"s7","level":"core","summary":"Keeping card numbers away from your servers by tokenising in the browser with the PSP SDK, which shrinks PCI DSS scope.","why":"Touching raw card data puts the whole system in full PCI scope with heavy audit cost. The integration choice decides compliance cost and UX control.","prereqs":["payment-system.html#c1","07-security.html#c6","07-security.html#c3"],"practice":"Use the Stripe test mode with Stripe Elements on a local page to create a payment with a test card. Confirm your server logs and database hold only tokens, and write which PCI SAQ level the design falls under and why."},{"id":"payment-system.html#c7","page":"payment-system.html","pageTitle":"Applied A · Payment processing","title":"Reconciliation & settlement","difficulty":4,"connects":["payment-system.html#c2","05-reliability-observability.html#c10"],"references":[{"url":"https://medium.com/airbnb-engineering/scaling-airbnbs-payment-platform-43ebfc99b324","title":"Airbnb — Scaling the payment platform"}],"stage":"s7","level":"advanced","summary":"Comparing your ledger with the PSP settlement file to match transactions and flag timing gaps and real mismatches.","why":"Without reconciliation, missing money is found by customers or auditors. It is how a payment system proves it is right.","prereqs":["payment-system.html#c2"],"practice":"Generate a sample ledger and a settlement CSV with planted differences (missing capture, amount mismatch, late settlement). Write a reconciler that sorts rows into matched, ours-only and theirs-only, and reports totals."},{"id":"payment-system.html#c8","page":"payment-system.html","pageTitle":"Applied A · Payment processing","title":"Webhook delivery & merchant notification","difficulty":3,"connects":["payment-system.html#c3","06-performance-scale.html#c7"],"references":[{"url":"https://www.svix.com/blog/webhooks-handbook/","title":"Svix — The Webhooks Handbook"},{"url":"https://stripe.com/docs/webhooks","title":"Stripe — Webhook best practices"}],"stage":"s7","level":"core","summary":"Notifying merchants of payment events by signed HTTP callbacks with retries, a dead-letter queue, and event ids for deduplication.","why":"Webhooks are at-least-once, so merchants must deduplicate, and unsigned webhooks can be spoofed. Delivery design decides integration reliability.","prereqs":["payment-system.html#c4","04-microservices-patterns.html#p3","01-distributed-systems.html#c9"],"practice":"Build a webhook sender that signs payloads with HMAC-SHA256, retries with backoff on non-2xx and dead-letters after N attempts. Build a receiver that verifies the signature and ignores duplicate event ids."},{"id":"order-fulfilment.html#c1","page":"order-fulfilment.html","pageTitle":"Applied B · Order fulfilment","title":"High-level architecture & order lifecycle","difficulty":4,"connects":["04-microservices-patterns.html#p5","04-microservices-patterns.html#p6"],"references":[{"url":"https://shopify.engineering/shopify-fulfillment-network","title":"Shopify — Building the Fulfilment Network"},{"url":"https://engineering.grab.com/scaling-grab-order-management","title":"Grab — Scaling order management"}],"stage":"s7","level":"core","summary":"The order lifecycle (place, assign, pick and pack, ship, transit, deliver) and how it splits into order, inventory, warehouse and tracking services.","why":"Service boundaries set where distributed transactions appear. Splitting too early or on the wrong lines makes every change cross-team.","prereqs":["04-microservices-patterns.html#p1","04-microservices-patterns.html#p6"],"practice":"Draw the order fulfilment architecture in 30 minutes, then write a short decision record on monolith versus services for a company with 3 engineers and for one with 60, naming the boundary you would split first."},{"id":"order-fulfilment.html#c2","page":"order-fulfilment.html","pageTitle":"Applied B · Order fulfilment","title":"Inventory reservation & oversell protection","difficulty":4,"connects":["01-distributed-systems.html#c6","06-performance-scale.html#c9"],"references":[{"url":"https://shopify.engineering/surviving-flashes-of-high-write-volume","title":"Shopify — Surviving flash sales"}],"stage":"s7","level":"advanced","summary":"Reserving stock at checkout with an atomic conditional update and a reservation expiry, then deducting at shipment, so two buyers cannot take the last item.","why":"Check-then-update races oversell stock, and flash sales turn one product into a hot key. This is a standard concurrency interview question.","prereqs":["order-fulfilment.html#c1","02-data-storage.html#c4","06-performance-scale.html#c9"],"practice":"Implement reservation with UPDATE ... WHERE physical - reserved >= qty and a 15-minute expiry job. Run 1,000 concurrent buyers for 10 units and confirm exactly 10 reservations; then compare with a Redis DECR gate."},{"id":"order-fulfilment.html#c3","page":"order-fulfilment.html","pageTitle":"Applied B · Order fulfilment","title":"Warehouse assignment & routing","difficulty":3,"connects":["order-fulfilment.html#c5","order-fulfilment.html#c2"],"references":[{"url":"https://engineering.zalando.com/posts/2022/06/logistics-optimization.html","title":"Zalando — Logistics optimisation"}],"stage":"s7","level":"core","summary":"Choosing which warehouse fulfils an order with a score over distance, cost, load and stock, and deciding when to split a shipment.","why":"Nearest-with-stock ignores cost and capacity and leaves some orders unfulfillable. It shows how to turn business rules into a scored decision.","prereqs":["order-fulfilment.html#c1","02-data-storage.html#c1"],"practice":"Seed 20 warehouses with locations and stock in PostGIS. Write a query that filters by stock and scores by distance, cost and load, and add a split-shipment rule with a cost threshold."},{"id":"order-fulfilment.html#c4","page":"order-fulfilment.html","pageTitle":"Applied B · Order fulfilment","title":"Order state machine & fulfilment saga","difficulty":4,"connects":["payment-system.html#c4","payment-system.html#c5"],"references":[{"url":"https://doordash.engineering/2020/08/14/order-management-system/","title":"DoorDash — Building the Order Platform"}],"stage":"s7","level":"advanced","summary":"The order state machine and the orchestrated fulfilment saga, including the rule for when cancel becomes a return.","why":"Order flows touch payment, stock and warehouses; without a state machine and compensations, cancellations corrupt stock and refunds.","prereqs":["order-fulfilment.html#c2","04-microservices-patterns.html#p1","payment-system.html#c4"],"practice":"Extend the payment saga exercise into a fulfilment saga with reserve, capture, assign and ship steps. Cancel at each state and verify the right compensations run, and that cancel after shipping is refused."},{"id":"order-fulfilment.html#c5","page":"order-fulfilment.html","pageTitle":"Applied B · Order fulfilment","title":"Real-time tracking & geospatial indexing","difficulty":4,"connects":["04-microservices-patterns.html#p6","04-microservices-patterns.html#p3"],"references":[{"url":"https://www.uber.com/blog/h3/","title":"Uber — H3: hexagonal hierarchical spatial index"},{"url":"https://doordash.engineering/2022/03/29/how-doordash-designed-a-real-time-tracking-system/","title":"DoorDash — Real-time tracking"}],"stage":"s7","level":"advanced","summary":"Tracking scans as events into a search read model, with a hexagonal geospatial index (H3) for fast \"nearby\" lookups.","why":"Distance queries over every driver or parcel do not scale. A grid index and a separate read model keep tracking fast at high event rates.","prereqs":["order-fulfilment.html#c1","04-microservices-patterns.html#p9"],"practice":"Using the h3 library, index 100,000 random driver points in a city. Answer \"drivers within 2 km\" with a k-ring lookup and with a brute-force distance scan, and compare time and accuracy."},{"id":"order-fulfilment.html#c6","page":"order-fulfilment.html","pageTitle":"Applied B · Order fulfilment","title":"ETA estimation","difficulty":5,"connects":["order-fulfilment.html#c5","order-fulfilment.html#c3"],"references":[{"url":"https://www.uber.com/blog/deepeta-how-uber-predicts-arrival-times/","title":"Uber — DeepETA"},{"url":"https://shopify.engineering/predicting-delivery-date","title":"Shopify — Delivery date prediction"}],"stage":"s7","level":"advanced","summary":"Estimating delivery time from processing, transit and last mile, improved from a lookup table to lane statistics to a model.","why":"Point estimates that miss erode trust. It shows how to start simple and iterate instead of building an ML pipeline first.","prereqs":["order-fulfilment.html#c5","order-fulfilment.html#c3"],"practice":"Generate a synthetic shipments dataset by lane with delays. Build V1 (carrier SLA lookup) and V2 (p50 and p90 per lane over 30 days) in SQL, and measure how often each range contains the actual delivery date."},{"id":"order-fulfilment.html#c7","page":"order-fulfilment.html","pageTitle":"Applied B · Order fulfilment","title":"Returns & reverse logistics","difficulty":3,"connects":["payment-system.html#c2","order-fulfilment.html#c2"],"references":[],"stage":"s7","level":"core","summary":"The return flow as its own state machine: authorise, ship back, inspect, restock or dispose, and refund at a chosen point.","why":"Returns tracked informally leak money and stock. Refund timing is a business risk decision that the design must support.","prereqs":["order-fulfilment.html#c4","payment-system.html#c2"],"practice":"Model a return state machine and its ledger postings. Implement refund-on-ship for trusted customers and refund-on-inspect for new ones, and verify stock and the ledger after a restock and after a disposal."},{"id":"order-fulfilment.html#c8","page":"order-fulfilment.html","pageTitle":"Applied B · Order fulfilment","title":"Event-driven tracking pipeline (CQRS)","difficulty":4,"connects":["04-microservices-patterns.html#p3","04-microservices-patterns.html#p6"],"references":[{"url":"https://doordash.engineering/2020/09/22/building-doordash-order-tracking/","title":"DoorDash — Building real-time order tracking"},{"url":"https://engineering.deliveryhero.com/event-driven-architecture/","title":"Delivery Hero — Event-driven architecture"}],"stage":"s7","level":"advanced","summary":"A CQRS tracking pipeline: scan events into Kafka and an event log, projected into a denormalised Elasticsearch timeline per order.","why":"Separating the write and read paths lets each scale, but the read side lags. Knowing where that lag is acceptable is the key judgement.","prereqs":["order-fulfilment.html#c5","04-microservices-patterns.html#p6"],"practice":"Build a projector that consumes tracking events from Redpanda into OpenSearch as one timeline document per order. Measure the lag at 5,000 events per second, then delete the index and rebuild it by replaying from offset 0."},{"id":"lakehouse-platform.html#c1","page":"lakehouse-platform.html","pageTitle":"Applied C · Lakehouse platform","title":"Requirements, constraints and the shape of the answer","difficulty":2,"connects":["lakehouse-platform.html#c5","lakehouse-platform.html#c6","lakehouse-platform.html#c8","05-reliability-observability.html#c1"],"references":[],"stage":"s7","level":"core","summary":"Turning a multi-entity analytics brief into requirements: which numbers must match, how fresh, who sees what, and which way each network allows connections.","why":"The non-functional numbers decide the architecture. Starting from tools instead of constraints is the most common failure in platform design and interviews.","prereqs":["08-data-platforms.html#c1","08-data-platforms.html#c4"],"practice":"Take a fictional three-company group with separate ERPs. In 45 minutes, write the questions you would ask, the numbers you would assume (volumes, freshness, tolerance), and the three constraints that shape the design."},{"id":"lakehouse-platform.html#c2","page":"lakehouse-platform.html","pageTitle":"Applied C · Lakehouse platform","title":"Version A, lean: push agents, Parquet, DuckDB and dbt","difficulty":3,"connects":["lakehouse-platform.html#c5","lakehouse-platform.html#c6","lakehouse-platform.html#c8","06-performance-scale.html#c7"],"references":[{"url":"https://duckdb.org/docs/","title":"DuckDB documentation"},{"url":"https://docs.getdbt.com/","title":"dbt documentation"}],"stage":"s7","level":"core","summary":"The lean design: push agents landing Parquet in object storage, one VM running DuckDB and dbt, and gold tables copied to Postgres for BI.","why":"When data fits on one machine, one machine is cheaper and simpler to run. Knowing when the lean design is enough is a key architect skill.","prereqs":["lakehouse-platform.html#c5","08-data-platforms.html#c2","08-data-platforms.html#c8"],"practice":"Build a mini Version A: Parquet files in MinIO, a dbt-duckdb project with staging, intermediate and mart models, and a final copy of one mart into Postgres. Time a full rebuild."},{"id":"lakehouse-platform.html#c3","page":"lakehouse-platform.html","pageTitle":"Applied C · Lakehouse platform","title":"Version B, scale and freshness: CDC, Kafka, Iceberg and Trino","difficulty":4,"connects":["02-data-storage.html#c8","04-microservices-patterns.html#p6","01-distributed-systems.html#c8","lakehouse-platform.html#c5"],"references":[{"url":"https://iceberg.apache.org/spec/","title":"Iceberg table spec"},{"url":"https://trino.io/docs/current/connector/iceberg.html","title":"Trino Iceberg connector"},{"url":"https://debezium.io/documentation/reference/stable/connectors/sqlserver.html","title":"Debezium SQL Server connector"}],"stage":"s7","level":"advanced","summary":"The scale and freshness design: SQL Server CDC through Debezium and Kafka into Iceberg tables, queried by Trino, with ClickHouse for hot dashboards.","why":"Minutes-level freshness and terabytes need a log-based pipeline, at a large cost in people and quiet failure modes. Knowing that cost stops over-building.","prereqs":["lakehouse-platform.html#c2","02-data-storage.html#c8","08-data-platforms.html#c5","04-microservices-patterns.html#p6"],"practice":"With Docker Compose, stream Postgres changes through Debezium into Kafka and into an Iceberg table (Kafka Connect Iceberg sink or a small Spark job), then query it from Trino. Note each component you would have to operate."},{"id":"lakehouse-platform.html#c4","page":"lakehouse-platform.html","pageTitle":"Applied C · Lakehouse platform","title":"Version C, managed: Microsoft Fabric and OneLake","difficulty":3,"connects":["lakehouse-platform.html#c2","lakehouse-platform.html#c8","05-reliability-observability.html#c7","07-security.html#c4"],"references":[{"url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/","title":"Microsoft Fabric documentation"}],"stage":"s7","level":"core","summary":"The managed design: Microsoft Fabric with OneLake, Delta tables, pipelines and Power BI Direct Lake under one capacity.","why":"Buying a platform trades control and cost shape for fewer engineers. Capacity throttling and lock-in are the risks to name.","prereqs":["lakehouse-platform.html#c2","08-data-platforms.html#c4","07-security.html#c4"],"practice":"On a Fabric trial (or from the documentation alone), sketch Version C for a sample dataset: which items you create, which identities read and write each layer, and what happens to every workload when the capacity throttles."},{"id":"lakehouse-platform.html#c5","page":"lakehouse-platform.html","pageTitle":"Applied C · Lakehouse platform","title":"Getting data out of a network that allows no inbound connections","difficulty":4,"connects":["01-distributed-systems.html#c8","03-apis-contracts.html#c9","02-data-storage.html#c8","07-security.html#c5"],"references":[{"url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/relational-databases/track-changes/about-change-tracking-sql-server","title":"SQL Server change tracking"},{"url":"https://fastapi.tiangolo.com/","title":"FastAPI"}],"stage":"s7","level":"advanced","summary":"Extracting data from a network that allows no inbound connections with an outbound push agent that authenticates with its own certificate and lands idempotent batches.","why":"Many real sources sit behind firewalls you do not control. Inverting the connection direction, with idempotent and checksummed landing, is the reusable answer.","prereqs":["lakehouse-platform.html#c1","08-data-platforms.html#c6","03-apis-contracts.html#c9","07-security.html#c5"],"practice":"Write a push agent that reads changed rows from a local Postgres, writes a Parquet batch with a checksum and a deterministic batch id, and uploads it over HTTPS to a small ingest API. Kill it mid-upload and show the retry lands exactly one batch."},{"id":"lakehouse-platform.html#c6","page":"lakehouse-platform.html","pageTitle":"Applied C · Lakehouse platform","title":"Proving the numbers: reconciliation, contracts and health","difficulty":4,"connects":["payment-system.html#c7","05-reliability-observability.html#c1","03-apis-contracts.html#c3","lakehouse-platform.html#c5"],"references":[{"url":"https://docs.getdbt.com/","title":"dbt documentation"}],"stage":"s7","level":"advanced","summary":"Making the platform prove its numbers on every load: source control totals computed with the batch, exact reconciliation in gold, contracts and health status.","why":"Finance judges the platform by whether its pack matches the ERP. A platform that cannot prove that will not be trusted, however fast it is.","prereqs":["lakehouse-platform.html#c5","08-data-platforms.html#c9"],"practice":"Extend the push agent to compute control totals per day and account in the same snapshot as the batch. Reconcile them against gold in exact decimal, plant a one-cent difference, and show the report names the account and day."},{"id":"lakehouse-platform.html#c7","page":"lakehouse-platform.html","pageTitle":"Applied C · Lakehouse platform","title":"Security and governance: identity, RLS for BI and the AI assistant, POPIA","difficulty":4,"connects":["07-security.html#c4","07-security.html#c3","03-apis-contracts.html#c8","07-security.html#c8"],"references":[{"url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/relational-databases/security/row-level-security","title":"SQL Server row-level security"}],"stage":"s7","level":"advanced","summary":"One identity provider, one entitlement table enforced at every door (BI, SQL, AI assistant), workload identities, and privacy obligations such as POPIA.","why":"Row-level security usually breaks at the door nobody checked, often the AI assistant. A single entitlement source enforced everywhere prevents leaks across entities.","prereqs":["lakehouse-platform.html#c1","07-security.html#c4","03-apis-contracts.html#c8","08-data-platforms.html#c10"],"practice":"Create an entitlement table mapping users to entities. Enforce it with Postgres row-level security and in a small API tool that an assistant would call on behalf of a user. Test that one user cannot reach another entity's rows through either path."},{"id":"lakehouse-platform.html#c8","page":"lakehouse-platform.html","pageTitle":"Applied C · Lakehouse platform","title":"Choosing between them: trade-offs and the triggers to move","difficulty":3,"connects":["lakehouse-platform.html#c2","lakehouse-platform.html#c3","lakehouse-platform.html#c4","02-data-storage.html#c9"],"references":[],"stage":"s7","level":"advanced","summary":"Comparing the lean, scale and managed designs on cost, freshness, effort, lock-in and failure mode, and naming the trigger to move between them.","why":"Architects are judged on the trade-off and on knowing when the answer changes. A design with no stated triggers ages badly.","prereqs":["lakehouse-platform.html#c2","lakehouse-platform.html#c3","lakehouse-platform.html#c4","02-data-storage.html#c9"],"practice":"Write a one-page decision record choosing between the three versions for a given set of numbers. Include a scoring table, how each fails, the measurable trigger that would move you, and the seam that makes moving cheap."}]};
